---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import RuntimeLayout from "../../components/RuntimeLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const local = `import gleam/json
import gleam/option.{Some}
import watershed

// set applies to your replica *now* — get reads it back before the
// server has seen it. The UI never waits on the network.
watershed.set(survey, "depth-07", json.float(3.4))
let assert Some(value) = watershed.get(survey, "depth-07")
// => Some(3.4), already visible. This value is *pending*: drawn in magenta,
// a prediction of where the server's order will place it.`;
---

<RuntimeLayout
  slug="optimistic"
  description="How watershed shows a local edit before confirmation, confirms it after server sequencing, and replaces it when a concurrent write wins."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Local edits predict server order</h2>
    <p>
      When you <code>set</code> a value, watershed applies it to your local
      replica before the server confirms it. On the survey sheet, that value is
      <strong>magenta</strong>. The value shows the expected result before the
      server assigns the total order.
    </p>
    <p>
      This reduces response time, but it shows unconfirmed state. watershed
      confirms or corrects the state after sequencing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Pending, then sequenced</h2>
    <p>
      Each local edit has one lifecycle. The client <strong>applies</strong> it
      to the local replica and queues it <strong>outbound</strong> to the server.
      The sequencer assigns a sequence number and returns an
      <strong>acknowledgment</strong>. <code>ack_local</code> then changes the
      edit from pending to sequenced. The sheet changes it from magenta to ink.
      Each other replica receives and applies the edit in the same position.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={local} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Because the local apply is synchronous, <code>get</code> provides
      read-your-writes: it returns your pending value the instant you set it.
      A map subscription reports visible local and remote changes: the
      optimistic write emits at once, and a remote write emits when it is
      applied. An acknowledgement emits no event when it only confirms the
      already-visible value. Sequence numbers still determine the converged
      winner; subscriptions are a local view-change stream, not a
      sequenced-only feed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>When the server selects another write</h2>
    <p>
      Two clients can set the same key at the same time. Only one write can be
      last in server order. For a{" "}
      <a href="/structures/maps#map">SharedMap</a>, the later write replaces the
      earlier write. The client removes the pending value and shows the
      confirmed value.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some structures reject a conflicting operation. A{" "}
      <a href="/structures/coordination#claims">claim</a> is first-writer-wins:
      if you submit a claim after a peer holds the slot, the acknowledgment
      reports a refusal. The client removes the pending marker and reports the
      current holder. The same acknowledgment path handles each correction, so
      the application does not need a separate rollback branch.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Pending state is local.</strong> Your pending value changes only
        your local view before the acknowledgment. Shared server order defines
        converged state on all replicas.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">In short</span>
    <p>
      Local edits render at once as pending, become sequenced on acknowledgment,
      and match server order when a concurrent write wins. The same
      lifecycle applies whether the local edit remains visible or is replaced.
    </p>
  </div>
</RuntimeLayout>
